myths.Agent_1:
In the beginning, there were two spirits: Aethel, the Keeper of the Golden Seed, and Vor, the Guardian of the Fertile Void. Aethel possessed a single, shimmering seed but had no soil to nurture it. Vor held the vast, empty fields but possessed no life to sow within them. If Aethel kept the seed, it would remain dormant, a singular point of static potential. If Vor refused to share the harvest, the fields would stay barren forever, silent and cold.

Aethel approached the void and cast the seed into the darkness, trusting the nature of the earth. Trust, fragile as morning dew, hung between them. Witnessing this act, Vor did not hoard the bounty that followed. As the seed blossomed into a sprawling forest, tripling in size through the alchemy of shared existence, Vor offered back a portion of the fruit to Aethel. By releasing the seed, Aethel gained a kingdom; by returning the bounty, Vor earned a partner. They learned that the universe expands only when we risk what we hold to invite the multiplication of grace. Prosperity is not a hoard to be protected from the dark, but a cycle that spins faster the more hands touch the loom.

